<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Storting</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Stortinget</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Storting is the parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsvoll">Eidsvoll</a> Constitution of 17 May 1814. In November 1814 it accepted the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xiii-of-sweden">Charles XIII of Sweden</a> as King of Norway while keeping the constitution. In 1905 it declared the union with Sweden dissolved. It is a unicameral assembly that meets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/storting.jpg" alt="Storting" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GlowstoneUnknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2025_Norwegian_Storting.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The parliament in its present form was first constituted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsvoll">Eidsvoll</a> in 1814, but its origins reach back to the allting of the 9th century, a type of thing, or common assembly of free men in Germanic societies, which met at a thingstead under lawspeakers. Such assemblies were gradually formalised and gained the backing and authority of the Crown. As oral laws were codified and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> unified in the 10th century, lagtings, or law things, became superior regional assemblies.</p><p>In the mid-13th century the regional assemblies, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frostating">Frostating</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulating">Gulating</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsivating">Eidsivating</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgarting">Borgarting</a>, were amalgamated and the law set down under King Magnus the Lawmender. This jurisdiction remained significant until Frederick III proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> in 1660, ratified by the King Act of 1665, which became the constitution of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> until 1814.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Storting has always been de facto unicameral, but until a constitutional amendment of 2009 it was de jure bicameral. After an election it elected a quarter of its members to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-aland">Lagting</a>, a revising chamber, and the other three quarters made up the Odelsting. The Lagting's composition came to follow the Odelsting's so closely that passing a bill in it was mostly a formality. Parliamentarianism was established in 1884. The Storting building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> has been in use since 5 March 1866.</p><p>On 27 June 1940, during the German occupation, the presidium signed an appeal to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-vii">Haakon VII</a> asking him to abdicate. In September 1940 the representatives voted 92 to 53 in favour of the results of negotiations between the presidium and the German occupation authority, but directives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> obstructed the agreement. The number of seats has varied, from 114 in 1882 to 150 in 1921 and 169 since 2005.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Storting is elected every four years by party-list proportional representation, a system adopted in 1919 in place of single-member districts. It always serves its full term, as the constitution does not allow snap elections. A bill goes through a standing committee and up to three readings, then to the King in Council for royal assent. Articles 77-79 of the constitution let the king withhold assent, a right no Norwegian monarch has used since the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> ended in 1905.</p><p>The assembly is led by a president and five vice presidents, who form the presidium. Parliament appoints the Office of the Auditor General and the Parliamentary Ombudsman, as well as the five members of the Norwegian Nobel Committee that awards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Storting” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The constitution adopted at Eidsvoll on 17 May 1814, among the most liberal of its time, created the <b>Storting</b>, Norway's parliament; parliamentary government was established in 1884 and women won the vote on equal terms in 1913.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1905, when the <b>Storting</b>'s declaration of 7 June dissolved the union with Sweden and the two countries came close to war that autumn, Swedish socialists sang &quot;Ja, vi elsker dette landet&quot; in support of the Norwegians' right to secede, while their leaders Hjalmar Branting and Zeth Höglund…</blockquote><a href="/songs/ja-vi-elsker-dette-landet">Ja, vi elsker dette landet</a></li><li><blockquote>The constitution, inspired by the American and French revolutions, divided power between king, an elected parliament, the <b>Storting</b>, and independent courts, and gave the vote to a relatively broad group of men.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-norway-1814">Kingdom of Norway (1814)</a></li><li><blockquote>After a short war in the summer of 1814, the Convention of Moss allowed Norway to keep its constitution, and the <b>Storting</b> elected Charles XIII of Sweden as king of Norway.</blockquote><a href="/state/sweden-norway">Sweden-Norway</a></li></ul></section>
